id: rule-while-good
title: A good invariant
topic: Loop
example-of: WHILE
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule WHILE to derive this triple. The conclusion of the rule
  has the invariant $I$ as precondition and $I && !B$ as postcondition,
  so here the invariant is the precondition $x >= 0$.

given:
  Loop condition B: $x > 0$

pre: x >= 0
post: x >= 0 && !(x > 0)

scheme: WHILE
  Preservation: {«I» && «x > 0 : B»} x := x - 1 {«I»}
  ---
  {«I»} while x > 0 do x := x - 1 {«I» && «!(x > 0) : ¬B»}

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while x > 0 do {
    {I && x > 0}
    {R1}
    x := x - 1     // b
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE
focus: while
text:
  The rule WHILE uses the precondition of its conclusion as the
  invariant.
set: I = x >= 0

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x >= 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  $P$ and $I$ are the same formula.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «x > 0 : B»} x := x - 1 {«I»}
  We substitute the variable $x$ with $x - 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 1, I)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- x - 1]     // I is x >= 0
  = x - 1 >= 0               // x replaced by x - 1
  = x >= 1                   // add 1 to both sides
set: R1 = x >= 1

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && x > 0, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «x > 0 : B» ==> «R1»
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 1
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is the same as $x >= 1$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(x > 0), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(x > 0) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: x >= 0 && !(x > 0) ==> x >= 0 && !(x > 0)
why:
  The two formulas are the same: $Q$ is $I && !B$. When the loop stops,
  $x = 0$.
